{"id":"h161","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"Photoelectrons may be emitted from sodium (phi = 2.36 eV) even for light intensities as low as 10^-8 W/m^2. Calculate classically how much time (in seconds) the light must shine to produce a photoelectron of kinetic energy 1.00 eV. Return the numeric value.\nIs the correct answer \"530000000.0\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The value 5.3×10⁸ s could arise from a different effective atomic area or energy budget.","confidence":0.3,"lean":0.4,"theta":0.4712,"phi":0.6559},{"text":"Textbook versions leave the target area to the solver, so the correct number depends on conventions.","confidence":0.5,"lean":0.3,"theta":0.7854,"phi":0.9897},{"text":"The electron must absorb at least φ + KE = 3.36 eV ≈ 5.38×10⁻¹⁹ J.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-2.8107},{"text":"The effective atomic cross-section is about π(0.1 nm)² ≈ 3.1×10⁻²⁰ m².","confidence":0.75,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.1781,"phi":-3.0465},{"text":"The absorbed power is about 3.1×10⁻²⁸ W.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.2,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.0411},{"text":"The required time is about 1.7×10⁹ s, roughly 50 years.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.0062},{"text":"Using only the 1.00 eV kinetic energy gives about 5.1×10⁸ s, close to but not equal to 5.3×10⁸ s.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.8145},{"text":"5.3×10⁸ s does not follow from the natural setup with standard assumptions.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.3352,"phi":2.5219},{"text":"The classical prediction of a delay of tens of years contradicts the observed near-instant emission.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.0062}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j"